! **A force with spin-orbit coupling and an ultrasoft dataset**, which is where
! the spinor force acquires its Pulay term.
!
! `pw_spinorbit/spinorbit.in`'s fcc platinum -- the case QE's own test-suite
! uses for `lspinorb` with a fully-relativistic ultrasoft dataset -- in a cell
! doubled along `a1`, so that there are two atoms and one of them can be pushed
! off its site. It sits 0.03 of the doubled vector away, which is 0.32 bohr.
!
! The dataset is ultrasoft, so `S` is not the identity and the orthonormality
! constraint carries the *spinor* metric `qq_so`: a complex 2x2 matrix in spin
! space where a collinear ultrasoft run has a real `qq`. That term is what
! `h4-noncolin-force.in` cannot test, its H.pz-vbc having no augmentation
! charge at all. `pt2-soc-paw-force.in` is the same cell with PAW.
!
! The k-grid is **unshifted**, unlike the one-atom file this comes from: an
! unshifted Monkhorst-Pack grid is closed under the point group, which is what
! the wedge-against-`nosym` check on the force needs.
!
! conv_thr is 1e-10 for `si2-nc-force.in`'s reason: QE's printed force carries
! `force_corr`, which corrects a density that stopped short of the fixed point
! and has no counterpart in a force obtained by differentiating the energy *at*
! the fixed point.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
    tprnfor = .true.
    tstress = .true.
    verbosity = 'high'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 0, celldm(1) = 7.42,
    nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    lspinorb = .true.,
    noncolin = .true.,
    starting_magnetization = 0.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'mp', degauss = 0.02,
    ecutwfc = 30.0, ecutrho = 250.0,
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
CELL_PARAMETERS alat
  -1.000000000000  0.000000000000  1.000000000000
   0.000000000000  0.500000000000  0.500000000000
  -0.500000000000  0.500000000000  0.000000000000
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Pt  195.08  Pt.rel-pz-n-rrkjus.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 Pt 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Pt 0.53 0.00 0.00
K_POINTS AUTOMATIC
 2 4 4 0 0 0
